{
    "background_story": "Westin was a visionary architect known for his innovative and eclectic designs. His magnum opus, the Octavia Building, was a marvel of modern architecture, featuring an octangular structure that seemed to defy gravity. The building's unique shape was not just aesthetically pleasing, but also served a functional purpose, allowing for maximum natural light and ventilation. Westin's use of sustainable materials and cutting-edge technology made the Octavia Building a model for green architecture. As his reputation grew, so did his fascination with the octangular shape, which he incorporated into many of his subsequent designs. Westin's work was not just about creating functional spaces, but about pushing the boundaries of what was thought possible, and the octangular shape became his signature motif.",
